[109.312834, "o", "echo \"=== final state ===\"; ls -d /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info 2>&1\r\npython3 -c \"import pip, pip._internal.cli.main; print('pip import chain OK')\" 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /tmp/piptest /tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.bak; echo cleaned echo \"…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\nFixed. `pip` works again.\r\n\r\n**What was actually wrong.** Not the whole Python install — only pip's code was missing. In `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/` there was `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` (the metadata) but no `pip/` package directory. The `pip`, `pip3` and `pip3.13` launchers in `/usr/local/bin/` were intact and still did `from pip._internal.cli.main import main`, so every invocation died with:\r\n\r\n```\r\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\r\n```\r\n\r\nThat message is misleading — it reads like \"pip isn't installed\" when in fact pip's *metadata* said it was. The interpreter, stdlib and `sys.path` we"]
[109.312865, "o", "re all healthy; `PYTHONPATH` was clean; there was no conflicting install shadowing it.\r\n\r\n**The fix.** Python ships a repair path for exactly this, and it needs no network: `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` — the exact version whose metadata was left behind.\r\n\r\nThe catch is worth knowing, because it's the trap here: `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` **does nothing** in this state. It sees the orphaned `dist-info`, concludes \"Requirement already satisfied: pip in … (24.3.1)\", and exits successfully — which is why the first attempt changed nothing. I moved the stale `dist-info` aside (backed up first), then:\r\n\r\n```\r\npython3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip\r\n→ Processing ./tmpXXXX/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl\r\n→ Successfully installed pip-24.3.1\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Verified, not assumed:**\r\n- `import pip` and `pip._internal.cli.main` both import cleanly\r\n- `pip -V` and `pip3 -V` → `pip 24.3.1 … (python 3.13)`\r\n- `python3 -m pip check` → no broken requirements\r\n- A"]
[109.31287, "o", " real install (`six`) into a throwaway `--target` succeeded, then was removed\r\n\r\n**Two things to flag:**\r\n\r\n1. I restored the version that was already meant to be there (24.3.1, matching the existing metadata and launchers) rather than upgrading to latest. That keeps the repair minimal and offline. Upgrading is now just `python3 -m pip install --upgrade pip` if you want it.\r\n\r\n2. **The deletion is unexplained.** Files don't vanish on their own — the usual causes are an interrupted upgrade, or a `pip uninstall pip` / `rm -rf` run by a package manager script mid-uninstall. If it was an interrupted upgrade, expect possible collateral damage in other packages; `pip check` is clean and nothing else was installed, so in this image nothing else was hit. Since it's a system-wide install as root, it's worth a glance at what ran around Oct 31 if this recurs.\r\n\r\n**On your actual workload:** `/app` is not a git repository — no `.git`, no tracked files, just `.gnomon/` and `.gnomon-audit/`. So there was no project her"]
[109.312877, "o", "e whose dependencies needed pip. If you were trying to install packages *for a project*, prefer a venv (`python3 -m venv .venv`) over the system install — it would have kept this breakage confined, and pip's own root warning points the same way.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=16 surface=c8c9507b08fa\r\n"]
